                "dc.date.enforcement_date" : [ {
                  "value" : "01-04-2010",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.date.issued" : [ {
                  "value" : "2009-08-26",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.date.last_modified" : [ {
                  "value" : "2019-12-02",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.description.abstract" : [ {
                  "value" : "Summary of the  Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009 \nThe Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009, ensures free and compulsory elementary education for children aged 6-14, emphasizing accessibility, quality, and protection against discrimination, with specific provisions for disadvantaged groups and children with disabilities.\n Key Provisions \n1. Infrastructure Norms \n•\tBarrier-Free Access: Mandatory for all schools to ensure inclusivity. \n•\tSeparate Toilets for Boys and Girls: Essential for hygiene and dignity. \n•\tSafe and Adequate Drinking Water Facility: Accessible to all children. \n•\tKitchen for Mid-Day Meals: Ensuring nutritional support within the school premises.\n•\tPlayground and Secured Fencing: For safety and recreational purposes. \n•\tOperational Norms: Minimum Working Days/ Instructional Hours: 200 working days for 1st to 5th class, with 800 instructional hours. 220 working days for 6th to 8th class, with 1,000 instructional hours. \n•\tTeacher Working Hours: 45 hours per week, including preparation time. \n•\tTeaching-Learning Equipment, Library, and Play Material: To be provided as required for each class. \n2. Transfer Rights and Support: Inter/Intra-State Transfers: Facilitated with immediate transfer certificates and no admission delays. \n•\tSpecial Training for Age-Appropriate Admissions: To integrate children into their age-appropriate classes seamlessly. \n3. Governance and Amendments: \n•\tParliamentary and Legislative Oversight: For transparency and accountability. \n•\tPower to Remove Difficulties: Granted to the Central Government for smooth implementation. Key Points (Updated for Clarity): \n4. Core Objective: Free and Compulsory Education for 6-14 year olds. \n5. Responsibilities: Governments: Funding, Infrastructure, Teacher Training. *\n•\tLocal Authorities: Oversight, Attendance Monitoring, Special Provisions. \n6. Prohibitions: Against Capitation Fees, Admission Screening, and Corporal Punishment.",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.identifier.act_id" : [ {
                  "value" : "AC_CEN_9_9_00006_200935_1517807327595",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
